# Shell scripts and container entrypoints run on Linux hosts. Without this a
# Windows checkout commits CRLF and the remote shell fails on $'\r'.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
Dockerfile.* text eol=lf
Caddyfile text eol=lf
Caddyfile.* text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Markdown and JSON were omitted here for a long time on the grounds that line
# endings are cosmetic. They are not: a CRLF file made a multiline regex match
# nothing, and the validator that depended on it reported "No entries yet" for a
# file with three entries. The requirement is not that Git stops warning - it is
# that a Windows checkout, a Linux checkout and every parser in between see the
# same bytes and the same semantics.
*.md text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.cjs text eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf

# `.txt` was the extension that rule forgot, and `review.txt` is the file that
# proves it matters: it is parsed in nine places across check-agents.mjs,
# control-center.mjs, check-customizations.mjs, backup-session.mjs and
# engineering-hook.test.mjs, and with `core.autocrlf=true` and no attribute it
# was `eol: unspecified` and had accumulated genuinely mixed endings (2200 CRLF
# against 844 bare LF). That is the exact condition described above — a
# multiline regex matching nothing and a validator reporting "No entries yet"
# for a file that has entries — waiting to happen on the append-only audit log,
# where a parser silently seeing nothing is the worst possible failure.
#
# The working copy stays mixed until someone runs `git add --renormalize`; this
# attribute stops it getting worse and fixes it at the next commit. review.txt
# is append-only, so it must NOT be rewritten in place to tidy the bytes.
*.txt text eol=lf

# CS-65 AC1: "every tracked text file has an explicit eol attribute". Adding
# `*.txt` closed the file the ticket was written about; it did not close the
# criterion. `check-line-endings.mjs` derives its universe from this file, so an
# undeclared file could not be checked at all - it could not even be seen. The
# second half of that check now asks the inverse question directly of
# `git ls-files`, and it named 43 text files that had no attribute: every SQL
# migration applied to production, every SVG shipped to the browser, the config
# files parsed line by line, and this file itself.
#
# Binary files are deliberately absent below. They are exempt by content - a NUL
# byte near the start - not by being listed, so a new image needs no edit here
# and cannot be mistaken for an oversight.
*.sql text eol=lf
*.svg text eol=lf
*.xml text eol=lf
*.webmanifest text eol=lf
*.conf text eol=lf
*.inc text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf

# Dotfiles carry no extension, and several are parsed one line at a time, where
# a stray CR becomes part of the pattern rather than terminating it.
.gitignore text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.dockerignore text eol=lf
.prettierignore text eol=lf
.editorconfig text eol=lf
.nvmrc text eol=lf
.env.example text eol=lf
*.timer text eol=lf
